<svg viewBox="0 0 720 420" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="What consumes memory during finetuning">
  <text x="40" y="32" font-size="13.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)">Inference memory (forward pass only)</text>
  <!-- inference bar -->
  <rect x="40" y="44" width="300" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--bg-inset)" stroke="var(--line-strong)" />
  <text x="190" y="71" font-size="11.5" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">model weights — N params × bytes/param</text>
  <rect x="344" y="44" width="60" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--bg-raised)" stroke="var(--line)" />
  <text x="374" y="66" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)">+~20%</text>
  <text x="374" y="80" font-size="9.5" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)">activations/KV</text>
  <text x="420" y="71" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">13B model @ 2 bytes ≈ 26 GB × 1.2 = 31 GB</text>

  <text x="40" y="136" font-size="13.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)">Training memory (forward + backward pass)</text>
  <!-- training bars -->
  <rect x="40" y="148" width="200" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--bg-inset)" stroke="var(--line-strong)" />
  <text x="140" y="175" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">weights</text>
  <rect x="244" y="148" width="120" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--bg-raised)" stroke="var(--line)" />
  <text x="304" y="170" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)">activations</text>
  <text x="304" y="184" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-faint)">(can dwarf weights!)</text>
  <rect x="368" y="148" width="140" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="var(--warn-ink)" />
  <text x="438" y="175" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--warn-ink)">gradients</text>
  <rect x="512" y="148" width="168" height="44" rx="7" fill="var(--warn-soft)" stroke="var(--warn-ink)" />
  <text x="596" y="170" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--warn-ink)">optimizer states</text>
  <text x="596" y="184" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)">(Adam: 2 per trainable param)</text>

  <text x="40" y="222" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">gradients + optimizer states scale with TRAINABLE parameters — 1 gradient + 0–2 optimizer values each</text>

  <!-- example math -->
  <rect x="40" y="240" width="640" height="100" rx="10" fill="var(--bg-raised)" stroke="var(--line)" />
  <text x="58" y="264" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)">The 13B example (Adam, 2 bytes/value):</text>
  <text x="58" y="286" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">full finetuning → 13B trainable × 3 values × 2 bytes = <tspan font-weight="600" fill="var(--bad-ink)">78 GB</tspan> for gradients + optimizer alone (plus 26 GB weights)</text>
  <text x="58" y="306" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">1B trainable params (PEFT-style) → 1B × 3 × 2 bytes = <tspan font-weight="600" fill="var(--good-ink)">6 GB</tspan> — the whole point of parameter-efficient finetuning</text>
  <text x="58" y="326" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">activations trick: don't store, recompute — <tspan font-style="italic">gradient checkpointing</tspan> trades time for memory</text>

  <text x="40" y="372" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)">The other lever: fewer bits per value (quantization)</text>
  <text x="40" y="392" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">FP32 (4 bytes) → FP16/BF16 (2) → INT8 (1) → INT4 (0.5) · inference goes low; training needs mixed precision</text>
  <text x="40" y="410" font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-soft)">same bits ≠ same format: BF16 trades precision for range vs FP16 — loading a model in the wrong one degrades it</text>
</svg>
